explanation:
This is a mixed mechanics question.

First distinguish scalars from vectors: a vector needs magnitude and direction.

For the car:
• resultant force F = ma;
• constant-acceleration kinematics connects v, a and s;
• work done by a force is force × displacement in the force direction;
• power is energy transferred per unit time.

When resistive forces are present, the motor's output energy must supply BOTH the increase in kinetic energy and the work done against resistance. At constant speed, acceleration is zero but the engine may still deliver power because it must balance resistive forces.

Try the complete Cambridge question before opening the mark scheme.

Focus: Potential difference; electrical energy; circuit quantities.

For Chapter 8, keep the fundamental relationships separate:
• current is rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t;
• potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge: V = W/Q;
• electrical power can be written P = IV;
• for charge carriers, use I = nAvq when required.

If the question also involves resistance, resistivity or a circuit network, identify that as an additional chapter rather than forcing everything into Chapter 8.

After completing the question, open OFFICIAL MARK SCHEME and compare each line of your working with the Cambridge marking points.

